<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vale of York</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vale of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> is a lowland area of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> and the North York <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>, drained by the Ouse and its tributaries. Its fertile farmland made it the core of Roman, Anglian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlement around York, and the main road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> ran along it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vale-of-york.jpg" alt="Vale of York" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Director General of the Ordnance Survey, UK · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ordnance_Survey_Quarter-inch_sheet_3_England_North_East,_published_1921.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The vale is often taken to run from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tees">River Tees</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber Estuary</a>, but strictly it is the central part of that lowland, with the Vale of Mowbray to its north and the Humberhead Levels to its south, the low ridge of the Escrick moraine marking its southern edge; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> lies in its middle. Triassic and lower Jurassic rocks lie wholly masked beneath glacial till, sand and gravel, and two moraines left by the retreating ice cross it, the Escrick moraine and, 8 miles to the north, the York moraine running east to Sand Hutton. North of them lie the deposits of a glacial lake.</p><p>The Ouse and its tributaries, the Ure, the Nidd and the Foss, drain it southwards, with the Derwent joining from the east. Lying in the rain shadow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a>, it has less rain than the lands to the west, and cold air draining from the higher ground brings more fog and frost in winter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The drier land away from the river valleys was probably widely cleared for pasture and small-scale cropping before the Roman era. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> established their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eboracum">Eboracum</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and left villas, forts, signal stations and roads across the area. The vale suffered badly in the Harrying of the North, when King William I laid waste the northern counties to punish resistance to his conquest. In the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> manors and villages were founded with open fields, some of which lasted until the inclosure acts of the 18th century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> the Battle of Marston Moor was fought west of York.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fertile soils formed from glacial deposits are almost all under the plough, growing wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> beet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> dominates the vale's economy. The A1 and A19 and the East Coast Main Line between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> run through it from north to south, and its villages typically line a main street with houses of mottled pink brick and pantiled roofs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vale of York” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Elmet emerged after Roman rule ended as a British kingdom in the area of modern West Yorkshire, between the Pennines and the <b>Vale of York</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-elmet">Kingdom of Elmet</a></li></ul></section>
